<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlaxcalans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tlaxcalan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tlaxcalans were the people of Tlaxcala, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> confederation east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> that remained independent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. They were long-standing enemies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and fought repeated ritual wars with them. When the Spanish arrived in 1519 the Tlaxcalans became their most important allies, providing thousands of warriors for the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans.jpg" alt="Tlaxcalans" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilhelm Sievers (1860 - 1921) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aztec_Indians_Mexico_Tlaxcalan_Cortez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tlaxcaltecs are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> people who formed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puebla-tlaxcala-valley">Puebla-Tlaxcala Valley</a> during the Postclassic period, between about 1200 and 1521. The chronicles place their origin among the teochichimecs, one of seven Nahuatlaca lineages that emerged, according to tradition, from Chicomoztoc, the place of the seven caves. Around 1290 they reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> and founded Poyauhtlan on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, but conflicts with other groups drove them out. The Olmec-Xicallanca granted them land near the hill of Cuatlapanga, where they settled. They became a conglomeration of speakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomi</a> and Pinome, and formed the confederation of Tlaxcallan from four altepetl: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepeticpac">Tepeticpac</a>, Ocotelulco, Tizatlán and Quiahuiztlán.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Unlike the hereditary monarchies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and many neighbours, Tlaxcallan was governed by shared power among several noble houses, with no single supreme ruler, and scholars sometimes call it a republic. Its council and rotating military leadership gave continuity under constant war. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, beans, squash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomatoes</a> and maguey, which yielded a sweetener, the fermented drink pulque and fibre. Ethnohistorical sources describe a trade embargo of more than sixty years imposed by the Mexica Triple Alliance, though research on obsidian trade questions how effective it was. The Tlaxcaltecs were never conquered, and their wars with the Mexica were called xochiyaoyatl, flower wars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> arrived in September 1519, the Tlaxcaltec senate resolved to fight. Under the general Xicohténcatl Axayacatzin, tens of thousands of warriors fought the Spanish near Tecoac and Tzompantepec. Lords such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxixcatzin">Maxixcatzin</a> and Xicohténcatl the Elder then argued for an alliance, and the Tlaxcaltecs became the most numerous indigenous allies of the Spanish.</p><p>The Crown gave Tlaxcala the status of an &quot;Indian republic&quot;, with its own cabildo, perpetual exemption from tribute and recognition of its nobles as hidalgos. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-the-ottoman-empire">Capitulations</a> of 1591, 400 Tlaxcaltec families settled the northern frontier. After independence, Mexican national narratives often called the Tlaxcaltecs traitors, a view historians such as Federico Navarrete have challenged since the late 20th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tlaxcaltec” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By some estimates Cortés's army at the final siege numbered as many as 100,000 warriors, of whom fewer than 1,000 were Spaniards; the great majority were <b>Tlaxcalans</b>, Huexotzincas, Chalcans, Texcocan factions and others who took the opportunity to destroy the power that had dominated or threatened them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Hernán Cortés landed on the Gulf coast in 1519, founded Veracruz, and with the decisive help of indigenous allies, above all the <b>Tlaxcalans</b>, besieged Tenochtitlan, which fell on 13 August 1521 after a smallpox epidemic had already devastated its defenders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The patron god of the <b>Tlaxcalans</b> was Camaxtli, a god of the hunt and of war closely linked to Mixcoatl.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tlaxcala">Tlaxcala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Mexico, Cortés's victory depended on tens of thousands of indigenous allies, above all the <b>Tlaxcalans</b>, who had their own reasons to destroy the Mexica tribute state.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
